{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310835,"nonce":"O9Ea8NAAX0/uQFH1vI69o8t38TM=","title":"Pure-Haxe parallelism patterns status (native + wasm): what works (fork-join reduce, Arc\u003cMutex\u003e, Atomic work-stealing) vs the 4 remaining ...","message":"**GOAL (user's framing):** make Haxe concurrency \"carry its own weight\" — identical multi-threading on native AND wasm — so nue's hot paths can move off per-section FFI hops into the Rust runtime and live in portable Haxe. The atomics ([[perf_wasm_real_arc_mutex]] precursor c1d1d890) + in-guest wasm Arc/Mutex (778f11f0) + closure-capture fix ([[bugs_captured_method_call_in_loop_sigsegv]] 710aeba7) are the foundation.\n\n**WORKS today, verified identical native↔wasm (committed a4c41894, `examples/concurrency/Main.hx`, all PASS both targets):**\n1. **Banded fork-join + reduce** — Thread.spawn per disjoint index band, each returns an Int partial, joiner folds. The parallel_rows decomposition in pure Haxe.\n2. **Arc\u003cMutex\u003cT\u003e\u003e shared accumulator** — workers lock the same guarded class instance and mutate a field. Real exclusion (parking_lot native / in-guest 3-state futex on wasm). 4×100k → exactly 400000 both targets.\n3. **Atomic work-stealing cursor** — `var c:Atomic = Atomic.of(Box.init(0).asPtr())`; workers `c.fetchAdd(1)` to claim the next index lock-free. Each index handed out once.\n\n**coreType cross-thread capture — FIXED (commits 8b0d7c1f + 4399e3dc).** `var a = Atomic.of(cell.asPtr())` now infers a concrete Send type and crosses threads with NO annotation (D work-stealing v==400000 native+wasm). ROOT (workflow wf_e3d33b88, 5-agent): the static-method-call gate in `ast_lowering.rs` (~8943, `lower_call_expression`) fired only for `SymbolKind::Class`; `@:coreType` abstracts are `SymbolKind::Abstract`, so `Atomic.of`/`Box.init` skipped the static path, fell to the instance path with an invalid-typed placeholder, and `infer_builtin_method_type`'s no-underlying-Abstract arm (~15029) returned `dynamic_type()`. NOT runtime_mapping/PtrVoid dyn-boxing (that only feeds IR/codegen, registers I64). FIX = 4 additive edits (gate accepts Abstract + class_methods registration for abstract pre-pass + resolve_class_method_symbol Strategy 2b qname-suffix + resolve_type_to_class_symbol_inner Abstract arm). trait_checker already grants Send to no-underlying coreType. Also made `Atomic\u003cT\u003e` GENERIC (mirror Ptr/Box/Arc). USER INSIGHT: dyn-boxing = runtime type-tag for opaque-FFI forwarding, ≠ rayzor.Box; pushed me off the runtime_mapping red herring onto generics + \"coreType shouldn't drop to Dynamic\".\n\n4. **Disjoint-band buffer write — WORKS (commit c031cd0a).** Workers write disjoint slices `out[i]=...` of a shared pre-sized Array, no lock, no copy = the zero-copy parallel_rows kernel shape. 5/5 native + wasm. The earlier \"Array unsafe in workers\" claim was STALE — it was the for-loop capture bug ([[bugs_captured_method_call_in_loop_sigsegv]] 710aeba7), NOT Array thread-safety. Workers can freely alloc/push/iterate Arrays internally too.\n\n5. **Return a HEAP object (Array/class) from Thread.join() — FIXED (commit 28c68338).** `var r = Thread.spawn(()-\u003esomeArray).join()` returned a corrupt pointer (`r.length`=heap addr → SIGSEGV); now correct native+wasm (Array len/elements + class fields). ROOT: `rayzor_thread_join` (concurrency.rs:282) ALWAYS boxes the closure i64 via `haxe_box_int_ptr`; the mir-wrapper auto-unbox (`hir_to_mir.rs` ~12857) resolved T from the receiver type_args but `maybe_unbox_for_extern_return`'s `_ =\u003e Some(value)` raw-passthrough arm (shared with un-boxed-handle methods like Arc.get) skipped heap T → box address used as object pointer. FIX = inline unbox (`haxe_unbox_int_ptr` → cast i64→Ptr(U8)) for a concrete heap-pointer T, **keyed on `runtime_func == \"Thread_join\"`** so only the boxing method changes (Arc.get + the other 8 maybe_unbox callers untouched); excludes Ptr(primitive). The wrong first attempt was the 10910 generic-stdlib auto-unbox path (Thread.join is a mir_wrapper → 12857, not 10910).\n\n**Channel marshaling — RESOLVED (no fix needed).** Channel\u003cInt\u003e AND Channel\u003cheapT\u003e (class) send/receive work CORRECTLY on native (42,7 / m1=42 m2=7). Unlike Thread.join, the runtime does NOT box: `rayzor_channel_send`/`receive` (concurrency.rs:605/664) push/pop the pointer AS-IS; boxing is symmetric at the call sites (send-box matches receive-unbox), so heap T just works. The Thread.join keyed fix is NOT needed for Channel. (Earlier \"W0020 on Channel\" was a MALFORMED test — `new Channel\u003cInt\u003e()` missing the required `capacity:Int` arg; the missing-arg surfaces as W0020 value-map-miss instead of a clean error — minor diagnostic-quality bug, separate.)\n\n6. **In-guest wasm Channel — LANDED (commit b61676cc).** rayzor.concurrent.Channel was UNIMPLEMENTED on the wasmtime path (no runtime-wasm symbol, no wasm_runner.rs fake → returned 0,0). Implemented in runtime-wasm/src/concurrent.rs: bounded MPMC ring buffer ([lock,head,tail,closed,capacity]+i32 slots), CAS spinlock guards head/tail/slots, blocking via a TIMED `memory.atomic.wait32` + best-effort notify (timed park yields CPU to a peer worker — a pure spin STARVES it — while the timeout guarantees re-check so a missed cross-instance notify can't deadlock). i32-ABI. WORKS: single-thread Channel\u003cInt\u003e/\u003cclass\u003e native+wasm (42,7 / m1=42,m2=7); cross-thread native (1000 items, sum 499500). Arc/Mutex/Atomic unbroken (4/4 wasm). NOTE the required `capacity:Int` ctor arg.\n\n**2 REMAINING GAPS (both likely the SAME wasm-worker-scheduling root):**\n- **Cross-thread BLOCKING Channel on wasm flaky/deadlocks.** A producer Thread.spawn worker doesn't PROGRESS while the main thread is busy on the channel (chx 1-2/8 PASS, rest 30s timeout). NOT the channel logic (native 5/5) and NOT the futex park reliability per se — spin (CPU-starve) → 6/6 deadlock, infinite futex → 2/5, timed futex → 1/8; the worker simply isn't running. Same class as the flash-attn worker race ([[bugs_wasm_flash_parallel_sharedmem_race]] — shadow-stack/dispatch). Likely shares a root with the WorkerPool hang below. Investigate: how rayzor_thread_spawn workers get CPU on wasm when main doesn't join (matmul/flash workers ran DURING a join; a fire-and-forget producer may never be dispatched).\n- **`WorkerPool.withForcedNodes(N)` hangs** — the Haxe pool wrapper (Thread.spawn + CpuTopology.bindToNode) deadlocks the forced-fanout path; raw Thread.spawn underneath is fine. Separate pre-existing bug in WorkerPool.hx / CpuTopology.\n\n**Methodology note:** nue's Haxe is mostly sequential orchestration; the parallelism is in the Rust side (nue-plugins + rayzor QTensor via `worker_pool.parallel_rows` — static bands, work-stealing w/ caller-assist, atomic join counters). These Haxe repros reproduce those shapes to validate the Haxe concurrency stack can replace the FFI.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `project_haxe_parallelism_patterns` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}